## Repository layout

```text
configs/                  Hydra YAML; generated files say so in their first line
  run.yaml                entry config of {{ cli }}-run: picks groups, holds this repo's choices
  defaults/run.yaml       every MainConfig setting with its default (generated)
{% if example %}
  signal/<kind>.yaml      each signal kind with its defaults, selected by signal=<kind> (generated)
{% endif %}
  hydra/default.yaml      Hydra writes nothing; the app writes runs/
src/{{ package }}/
  cli/                    one module per console script: the composition roots
  config.py               entry config schemas + GENERATED (files `make configs` writes)
{% if example %}
  signals/                example family: base.py, one module per kind in items/
  stats/                  example domain: summary.py decides, report.py writes
{% endif %}
  paths.py                repository paths, from the .project-root marker
tests/                    mirrors src/ module by module
  test_invariants.py      configs compose and are fresh, kinds build, import layers hold
  integration/            end to end, `-m integration`
scripts/                  exploratory or one-time runnables, never imported; rerun ones become cli/ commands
notebooks/                exploration only, outputs stripped; reference/ for third-party notebooks
.claude/                  settings.json enables the plugins; rules/ holds the template's rules
runs/ data/               gitignored
```

## Families

A family is a concept with several implementations chosen by a config `kind`. The `nuikit` plugin's `add-family` and
`add-family-item` skills walk through adding one.

{% if example %}
```text
src/{{ package }}/signals/
  base.py        Signal (attrs ABC), SignalConfig, SignalFactory (config_group = "signal")
  items/
    __init__.py  import_submodules(__name__)
    constant.py  @SignalFactory.register("constant") above @define
    gaussian.py  @SignalFactory.register("gaussian") above @define
```

```python
class GaussianSignalConfig(SignalConfig):
    """Settings of the `gaussian` signal."""

    mean: float = 0.0
    std: PositiveFloat = 1.0


@SignalFactory.register("gaussian")  # Outermost: attrs replaces the class below it.
@define
class GaussianSignal(Signal[GaussianSignalConfig]):  # The type argument binds Config.
    """Signal of independent normal draws."""

    def sample(self, n: int, *, rng: np.random.Generator) -> NDArray[np.float64]:
        return rng.normal(self.config.mean, self.config.std, size=n)
```

`SignalFactory.from_config({"kind": "gaussian", "std": 2})` validates against `GaussianSignalConfig` and builds the
item; `make configs` writes `configs/signal/gaussian.yaml`, the file `signal=gaussian` selects. `MainConfig.signal`
resolves its kind through `SignalFactory.validate`, so `signal=gausian` fails with exit code 2 and lists the
registered kinds. The family's `__init__.py` imports `items`, so importing the family registers its kinds;
`make configs` and the invariant tests import the whole package, so they see every family even before any code uses
it.
{% else %}
```text
src/{{ package }}/<family>/
  base.py        <Family> (attrs ABC), <Family>Config, <Family>Factory (config_group = "<family>")
  items/
    __init__.py  import_submodules(__name__)
    <kind>.py    @<Family>Factory.register("<kind>") above @define
```

```python
class SquareConfig(ShapeConfig):
    """Square settings."""

    side_m: float = Field(default=1.0, gt=0)


@ShapeFactory.register("square")  # Outermost: attrs replaces the class.
@define
class Square(Shape[SquareConfig]):  # The type argument binds Square.Config.
    """Axis-aligned square."""

    def area(self) -> float:
        return self.config.side_m**2
```

`ShapeFactory.from_config({"kind": "square", "side_m": 2})` validates against `SquareConfig` and builds the item;
`make configs` writes `configs/shape/square.yaml`, the file `shape=square` selects. The family's `__init__.py` imports
`items`, so importing the family registers its kinds; `make configs` and the invariant tests import the whole package,
so they see every family even before any code uses it.
{% endif %}

## Extending

| to add | create | and |
|---|---|---|
| a family | `src/{{ package }}/<family>/base.py` + `items/` | place it in the import-linter `layers`; `make configs` |
{% if example %}
| a signal | `src/{{ package }}/signals/items/<kind>.py` | `make configs`; select it with `signal=<kind>` |
{% endif %}
| an item | `src/{{ package }}/<family>/items/<kind>.py` | `make configs` |
| an entry config | a schema in `config.py` listed in `GENERATED` as `defaults/<command>.yaml`, and `configs/<command>.yaml` composing it | a `cli/<command>.py` and a console script |
| a setting | a field with a default on the schema, described only if its name leaves something open (DOC-7) | `make configs` |
{% if example %}

## Removing the example

Once the first real family exists, drop `signals` and `stats` from a clean working tree:

```bash
uvx copier update --trust --defaults --data example=false
```

Copier deletes the example's files and takes its lines out of `config.py`, `cli/run.py`, `configs/run.yaml`, the
import layers and these docs; resolve any conflicts it leaves (`*.rej`), then `make configs` and `make check`.
{% endif %}

## Tests

```bash
make test               # unit tests, a few seconds
make test-integration   # end to end, behind the integration marker
```

Fakes, not mocks (`unittest.mock` is banned by ruff). Each case covers a distinct behaviour; child processes and
servers only for what cannot be measured in process.

## Commits

Conventional Commits with the standard types (`feat fix docs refactor test build ci perf style chore revert`), an
optional scope, a lowercase imperative subject: one line, no articles, no body or trailers. Short-lived `<type>/<slug>`
branches, squash-merged. The commit-msg hooks check all of it. PR descriptions follow
`.github/pull_request_template.md`; `make github`, run once per repo, makes the squash commit the PR title alone.

## Deviations

None yet. A rule this repo can't follow yet goes here, with the reason. A rule that does not apply to it at all also
goes in `ignore` of the `[tool.conventions]` table in `pyproject.toml`, so the hooks skip it.
